[size=18][b]VNDB SciRanking: re-ranking VNDB with head-to-head comparisons[/b][/size]

Site: [url=https://vndb.mojimoon.top]vndb.mojimoon.top[/url] (English / Chinese, no account needed)
Source: [url=https://github.com/mojimoon/vndb]github.com/mojimoon/vndb[/url]

[b]The idea[/b]
The Bayesian rating averages everyone's votes, but an 8 from someone who gives everything 9 means something different from an 8 from someone who usually gives 6, and a niche title is rated by a different crowd than a popular one.

SciRanking only compares titles [i]through the same people[/i]. Every user who voted on two titles is one "game" between them: who did they rate higher, and where does each title sit in that user's own list (its sample percentile)? With today's dump that is about 8,000 titles with 30+ votes, 1.9M votes and 4.9M comparable pairs. Rating algorithms from sports ranking (Massey, Colley, Bradley–Terry, Elo, Markov chains, offence–defence, ...) then turn those games into one strength per title.

[b]The default ranking[/b]
The default "grand ranking" is a Borda count over 9 of these rankings (Massey and Difference on percentile margins, Colley, Bradley–Terry, Elo). They were chosen by splitting the voters into two random halves and keeping the methods whose two halves agree best once popularity is factored out. The result tracks popularity about as much as VNDB's own rating does (Spearman ρ with log vote count 0.38 vs. 0.34), and its top isn't taken over by titles with a few dozen votes. The experiments, including methods that were tried and dropped, are written up [url=https://github.com/mojimoon/vndb/blob/main/docs/ranking-experiments.md]here[/url]. All 41 rankings can also be browsed and compared side by side (up to 3 at once).

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mojimoon/vndb/main/docs/announcement/img/en/ranking.png[/img]

[b]What else is there[/b]
[b]-[/b] Ranking filters by developer, year, language, length, votes and rating; best per year; biggest disagreements with VNDB; 7-day movers; CSV export.
[b]-[/b] Title pages: vote distribution, where voters place the title within their own lists, votes and mean per year, rank history under each method. The ratings can be recomputed for a subset of voters (by list status, the voter's vote count, or how closely their votes follow VNDB / SciRanking).
[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mojimoon/vndb/main/docs/announcement/img/en/vn-ratings.png[/img]

[b]-[/b] Head-to-head between any two titles: how many common voters preferred each, plus the 10×10 matrix of their votes. Similar titles come with their head-to-head record.
[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mojimoon/vndb/main/docs/announcement/img/en/compare-vn.png[/img]

[b]-[/b] User pages for anyone with 5+ public votes on ranked titles (search by username): taste profile, correlation with VNDB and SciRanking, vote-vs-rating heatmaps, recommendations from similar users, and user-vs-user comparison.
[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mojimoon/vndb/main/docs/announcement/img/en/user.png[/img]

[b]-[/b] Reviews: public list notes (20+ characters), sortable by date, vote or where the voter places the title, filterable by list status.
[b]-[/b] Developer rankings and developer pages.

[b]Data[/b]
Everything comes from the public [url=https://vndb.org/d14]database dump[/url] (ODbL) and is refreshed daily. Only public votes are used, and users whose votes VNDB ignores are excluded, as on VNDB itself.

Feedback, bug reports and ideas are very welcome here or on GitHub.
